1
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:36,000
The old west meets the far east.

2
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:06,000
Shanghai Noon is a adventure, action, comedy film directed by Tom Dey.

3
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:36,000
Chon Wang, a clumsy imperial guard, trails Princess Pei Pei when she's
kidnapped from the Forbidden City and transported to America.

4
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:06,000
Wang follows her captors to Nevada, where he teams up with an unlikely partner, outcast
outlaw Roy O'Bannon, and tries to spring the princess from her imprisonment.

5
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:36,000
Chon Wang, played by Jackie Chan.

6
00:33:00,000 --> 00:33:06,000
Roy O'Bannon, played by Owen Wilson.

7
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:36,000
Princess Pei Pei, played by Lucy Liu.

8
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:06,000
Nathan Van Cleef, played by Xander Berkeley.

9
00:49:30,000 --> 00:49:36,000
Lo Fong, played by Roger Yuan.

10
00:55:00,000 --> 00:55:06,000
Imperial Guard, played by Yu Rongguang.

11
01:00:30,000 --> 01:00:36,000
Shanghai Noon is a 2000 martial arts western action comedy film directed by Tom Dey in his feature film debut,
written by Alfred Gough and Miles Millar, and starring Jackie Chan, Owen Wilson and Lucy Liu.

12
01:06:00,000 --> 01:06:06,000
It is the first entry in the Shanghai film series.

13
01:11:30,000 --> 01:11:36,000
The film, set in Nevada and other parts of the American West in the 19th century, is a juxtaposition of a Western with a kung fu action
film with extended martial arts sequences, as reflected by its title, which is a wordplay on the notable western High Noon.

14
01:17:00,000 --> 01:17:06,000
It also has elements of slapstick comedy and the "buddy film" genre, featuring two vastly different
heroes (a Chinese Imperial Guard and an American Western outlaw) who team up to stop a crime.

15
01:22:30,000 --> 01:22:36,000
The film premiered in Malaysia in May 19, 2000 and was
released in the United States on May 26, 2000.

16
01:28:00,000 --> 01:28:06,000
It received generally positive reviews from critics.

17
01:33:30,000 --> 01:33:36,000
A sequel, Shanghai Knights, was released in 2003, with
Chan and Wilson reprising their roles.

18
01:39:00,000 --> 01:39:06,000
The film explores themes of princess, rescue,
martial arts, native american, sioux.

19
01:44:30,000 --> 01:44:36,000
The tone is lighthearted.
